<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Chad Wills — AI-Augmented Analytics &amp; Data Quality</title>
<meta name="description" content="Quantitative analyst working at the intersection of analytics engineering, data quality, and AI-augmented practice.">
<meta name="author" content="Chad Wills">
<meta name="theme-color" content="#0f766e">
<meta property="og:type" content="profile">
<meta property="og:title" content="Chad Wills — AI-Augmented Analytics &amp; Data Quality">
<meta property="og:description" content="I own the reasoning, the validation, and the judgment. AI removes the mechanical bottleneck.">
<style>
  :root {
    --bg: #fbfbfa;
    --surface: #ffffff;
    --ink: #14171a;
    --ink-soft: #474e55;
    --ink-faint: #71787f;
    --rule: #e4e6e5;
    --rule-strong: #cfd3d2;
    --accent: #0f766e;
    --accent-ink: #0b5c56;
    --accent-soft: #e8f3f1;
    --chip-bg: #f2f4f3;
    --shadow: 0 1px 2px rgba(16,25,29,.045), 0 10px 26px -16px rgba(16,25,29,.13);
  }

  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    :root {
      --bg: #0d1012;
      --surface: #15181b;
      --ink: #eaedef;
      --ink-soft: #aeb6bc;
      --ink-faint: #868f96;
      --rule: #252a2e;
      --rule-strong: #353b40;
      --accent: #5eead4;
      --accent-ink: #7ff0dd;
      --accent-soft: #10302c;
      --chip-bg: #1c2124;
      --shadow: none;
    }
  }

  * { box-sizing: border-box; }
  html { -webkit-text-size-adjust: 100%; }

  body {
    margin: 0;
    background: var(--bg);
    color: var(--ink);
    font-family: ui-sans-serif, -apple-system, "Segoe UI", Roboto, "Helvetica Neue", Arial, sans-serif;
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.55;
    -webkit-font-smoothing: antialiased;
    text-rendering: optimizeLegibility;
  }

  .page {
    max-width: 62rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 3.5rem 1.5rem 4rem;
  }

  a { color: var(--accent-ink); text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule-strong); }
  a:hover { border-bottom-color: var(--accent); }
  a:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--accent); outline-offset: 3px; border-radius: 2px; }

  /* ---------- Chips ---------- */

  .chips { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .38rem; }

  .chip {
    font-size: .775rem;
    line-height: 1.35;
    padding: .26rem .62rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: var(--chip-bg);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .chip-key {
    background: var(--accent-soft);
    border-color: transparent;
    color: var(--accent-ink);
    font-weight: 570;
  }

  /* ---------- Header ---------- */

  header {
    padding-bottom: 1.6rem;
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--rule-strong);
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    font-size: .7rem;
    font-weight: 640;
    letter-spacing: .14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--accent-ink);
    margin: 0 0 .7rem;
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2.1rem, 5.5vw, 2.9rem);
    line-height: 1.05;
    letter-spacing: -.03em;
    font-weight: 700;
    margin: 0 0 .55rem;
  }

  .tagline {
    font-size: 1.06rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin: 0 0 1.15rem;
    max-width: 46rem;
  }
  .tagline strong { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 620; }

  .meta {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: .4rem 1rem;
    align-items: baseline;
    margin-top: 1.15rem;
    font-size: .875rem;
    color: var(--ink-faint);
  }
  .meta a { word-break: break-all; }

  /* ---------- Sections ---------- */

  section { margin-bottom: 2.6rem; }

  h2 {
    font-size: .73rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: .15em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-faint);
    margin: 0 0 1.1rem;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: .8rem;
  }
  h2::after {
    content: "";
    flex: 1;
    height: 1px;
    background: var(--rule);
  }

  p { margin: 0 0 .85rem; }
  p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

  .lede { font-size: 1.03rem; color: var(--ink-soft); max-width: 52rem; }
  .lede strong { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 620; }

  /* ---------- Operating model ---------- */

  .modes { display: grid; gap: .7rem; margin-bottom: .9rem; }
  @media (min-width: 48rem) { .modes { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); } }

  .mode {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-top: 2px solid var(--accent);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: .95rem 1.05rem;
  }
  .mode-name {
    font-size: .69rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: .12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--accent-ink);
    margin: 0 0 .35rem;
  }
  .mode p { margin: 0; font-size: .89rem; color: var(--ink-soft); }

  .governance {
    background: var(--accent-soft);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: .95rem 1.15rem;
    font-size: .9rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .governance p { margin: 0; }
  .governance b { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 640; }

  /* ---------- Work cards ---------- */

  .cards { display: grid; gap: .85rem; }
  @media (min-width: 58rem) { .cards { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); gap: .85rem; } }

  .card {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: 9px;
    padding: 1.15rem 1.25rem;
    box-shadow: var(--shadow);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
  }

  .tag {
    align-self: flex-start;
    font-size: .645rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: .1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--accent-ink);
    background: var(--accent-soft);
    padding: .2rem .5rem;
    border-radius: 3px;
    margin-bottom: .6rem;
  }

  .card h3 {
    font-size: 1rem;
    font-weight: 660;
    letter-spacing: -.012em;
    margin: 0 0 .45rem;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  .setup {
    font-size: .89rem;
    color: var(--ink-faint);
    font-style: italic;
    margin: 0 0 .7rem;
  }

  ul.b { list-style: none; margin: 0 0 .85rem; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: .42rem; }
  ul.b li {
    position: relative;
    padding-left: .95rem;
    font-size: .885rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  ul.b li::before {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: .58em;
    width: .3rem; height: .3rem;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--accent);
  }

  .outcome {
    margin-top: auto;
    background: var(--accent-soft);
    border-radius: 6px;
    padding: .55rem .75rem;
    font-size: .865rem;
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .outcome b {
    color: var(--accent-ink);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: .02em;
  }

  /* ---------- Skills ---------- */

  .skills { display: grid; gap: 1rem; }
  @media (min-width: 48rem) { .skills { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); gap: 1rem 2rem; } }

  .skill-group h3 {
    font-size: .715rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: .1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-faint);
    margin: 0 0 .45rem;
  }

  /* ---------- Principles ---------- */

  ul.principles { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: .55rem; }
  @media (min-width: 48rem) { ul.principles { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); gap: .55rem 2rem; } }
  ul.principles li {
    position: relative;
    padding-left: 1.05rem;
    font-size: .9rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  ul.principles li::before {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    left: 0; top: .6em;
    width: .34rem; height: .34rem;
    background: var(--accent);
    transform: rotate(45deg);
  }
  ul.principles b { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 640; }

  /* ---------- Recognition ---------- */

  ul.recognition { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: .6rem; }
  @media (min-width: 48rem) { ul.recognition { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); gap: .6rem 1.6rem; } }
  ul.recognition li {
    font-size: .885rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    padding-left: .9rem;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--accent);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  /* ---------- Footer ---------- */

  footer {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    padding-top: 1.1rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    font-size: .8rem;
    color: var(--ink-faint);
  }

  /* ---------- Print ---------- */

  @media print {
    :root {
      --bg: #fff; --surface: #fff; --ink: #000; --ink-soft: #1e2124; --ink-faint: #494d50;
      --rule: #c9cccd; --rule-strong: #9fa3a5; --accent: #0b5750; --accent-ink: #0b5750;
      --accent-soft: #f2f6f5; --chip-bg: #fff; --shadow: none;
    }
    body { font-size: 9.6pt; line-height: 1.4; }
    .page { max-width: none; padding: 0; }
    header { margin-bottom: 1.1rem; padding-bottom: .8rem; }
    h1 { font-size: 20pt; }
    .tagline, .lede { font-size: 10pt; }
    section { margin-bottom: 1.1rem; }
    h2 { margin-bottom: .6rem; }
    .cards { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: .5rem; }
    .modes { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: .4rem; }
    .card, .mode, .governance, .outcome {
      box-shadow: none;
      break-inside: avoid;
      page-break-inside: avoid;
    }
    .card { padding: .6rem .7rem; border-color: var(--rule); }
    .chip { border: 1px solid var(--rule); }
    a { color: #000; border-bottom: none; }
    h2, h3 { break-after: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; }
    footer { margin-top: 1rem; }
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>
<main class="page">

  <header>
    <p class="eyebrow">Analytics &middot; Data Quality &middot; AI-Augmented Practice</p>
    <h1>Chad Wills</h1>
    <p class="tagline">
      Quantitative Analyst at <strong>Asurion</strong>. I build the data quality, reporting, and
      automation layer that enterprise programs are measured on.
    </p>
    <div class="chips">
      <span class="chip chip-key">Data Quality Engineering</span>
      <span class="chip chip-key">Root-Cause Investigation</span>
      <span class="chip chip-key">BI as Code</span>
      <span class="chip chip-key">AI Enablement</span>
      <span class="chip chip-key">Pipeline Automation</span>
      <span class="chip chip-key">Executive Communication</span>
    </div>
    <p class="meta"><span>LinkedIn:</span> <a href="{{LINKEDIN_URL}}">{{LINKEDIN_URL}}</a></p>
  </header>

  <section>
    <h2>Profile</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      I make the numbers executives rely on defensible, and the systems behind them durable. The
      work usually starts as a question nobody can answer confidently &mdash; a metric that looks
      wrong, a field that is silently empty, a pipeline that fails without telling anyone &mdash;
      and ends as a documented root cause, a fix routed to the team that owns it, and a check that
      catches the next one automatically.
    </p>
    <p class="lede">
      <strong>AI is central to how I work, and it is not autocomplete.</strong> I own the domain
      reasoning, the validation, and the judgment; AI removes the mechanical bottleneck. The
      governance around it is what makes that speed safe to use.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How I work with AI</h2>
    <div class="modes">
      <div class="mode">
        <p class="mode-name">Investigative</p>
        <p>I set the hypothesis; AI writes the queries that test it. When the data contradicts me
        &mdash; often the most valuable outcome &mdash; I choose the next direction.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="mode">
        <p class="mode-name">Comprehension</p>
        <p>AI reads structured material at a volume I cannot. I validate it against domain
        knowledge and catch what is undocumented, misleading, or quietly wrong.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="mode">
        <p class="mode-name">Execution</p>
        <p>I define the spec; AI propagates it across files, formats, and layers. I own
        correctness, feed errors back, and close the loop.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="governance">
      <p><b>The governance layer is the part people underestimate.</b> Anything irreversible is
      gated on an explicit human decision. Checks fail closed rather than passing quietly. Queries
      are aggregate-first and privacy-safe by default. Findings are written down where they survive
      the session. Speed without those constraints is not an asset &mdash; it is just a faster way
      to be wrong.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Selected work</h2>
    <div class="cards">

      <article class="card">
        <span class="tag">Data Quality Platform</span>
        <h3>Automated data-quality and lineage auditing</h3>
        <p class="setup">Reporting trust depended on someone eventually noticing a number looked wrong.</p>
        <ul class="b">
          <li>Built a daily audit tracing recursive upstream lineage across every object the reporting dataset consumes, surfacing only genuine drift: schema change, row-count divergence, freshness lag.</li>
          <li>Re-architected it off any dependency on an individual's machine into a server-side pipeline publishing to a cloud lakehouse.</li>
          <li>Hardened it for production: settled-window logic suppresses alarms from loads still in flight; token refresh, write retries, and partial-wipe guards keep a mid-run failure from corrupting results.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="outcome"><b>Outcome</b> &mdash; silent upstream breakage became a detected, attributable finding, caught before a stakeholder ever saw a wrong number.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="card">
        <span class="tag">Investigation</span>
        <h3>Reframing the question before fixing the wrong thing</h3>
        <p class="setup">The most expensive failure in analytics is a confident investigation of the wrong cause.</p>
        <ul class="b">
          <li>Read the lineage before pulling data, then design aggregate-only queries that make each hypothesis falsifiable.</li>
          <li>Moved problems to entirely different owners: a client-blamed data gap traced to an internal pipeline that had never written the records at all; a climbing denial rate traced to a residual status code from a filter family corrected earlier.</li>
          <li>Reframed a "this count looks too low" escalation as a definitional expectation after reconciling the population end to end &mdash; and said so when the data disproved my own theory.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="outcome"><b>Outcome</b> &mdash; remediation lands on the real defect and the team that owns it, with evidence that survives challenge in the room.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="card">
        <span class="tag">AI Enablement</span>
        <h3>Turning expert workflows into reusable agent tooling</h3>
        <p class="setup">The workflows that made me effective lived in my head and ran only when I ran them.</p>
        <ul class="b">
          <li>Generalized request triage, backlog planning, data-quality briefing, and flow-architecture mapping into client-agnostic, configuration-driven agent skills.</li>
          <li>Built approval gates and advisor-only boundaries into the design, so nothing reaches a tracker or a channel without an explicit human decision.</li>
          <li>Added a usage-attribution layer reconciling tooling spend back to specific deliverables, leaving unmatched activity unassigned rather than forcing a match.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="outcome"><b>Outcome</b> &mdash; individual expertise became tooling colleagues can run, with the cost auditable instead of asserted.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="card">
        <span class="tag">BI as Code</span>
        <h3>Semantic models and reports as version-controlled text</h3>
        <p class="setup">Click-through report development cannot be reviewed, diffed, or trusted.</p>
        <ul class="b">
          <li>Author semantic models and report layouts directly as text: measures, calculated columns, relationship behavior, row-level security, and visual query bindings.</li>
          <li>Built repeatable transform scripts that apply a cleanup specification consistently instead of by hand.</li>
          <li>Reverse-engineered embedded queries out of published models into source-of-truth files so the logic can be reviewed, diffed, and shared.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="outcome"><b>Outcome</b> &mdash; reporting gained the rigor of the pipelines behind it, and an entire class of "someone clicked the wrong thing" errors disappeared.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="card">
        <span class="tag">Automation</span>
        <h3>Unattended, self-healing reporting operations</h3>
        <p class="setup">A daily refresh ritual burned analyst time and made fresh data depend on one person being at a desk.</p>
        <ul class="b">
          <li>Automated the full cycle on a schedule with logging, backups, retries with process cleanup, and chat notification on both success and failure.</li>
          <li>Diagnosed the failure modes that only appear in production: refreshes reporting success instantly because queries ran asynchronously, cloud sync holding files locked at exactly the scheduled hour, orphaned sessions, and dialogs blocking an unattended run.</li>
          <li>Traced a multi-day silent outage to sync contention rather than the obvious suspect, and fixed the cause instead of padding a timeout.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="outcome"><b>Outcome</b> &mdash; a manual ritual became an unattended, self-reporting service with no single-person dependency.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="card">
        <span class="tag">Executive Communication</span>
        <h3>Translating remediation into a decision leaders can make</h3>
        <p class="setup">Technical wins die in translation.</p>
        <ul class="b">
          <li>Build executive material as a connected argument &mdash; purpose, risk, change, proof, durability, cost, limits &mdash; with every title stating a business takeaway rather than a topic.</li>
          <li>Insist on labeled estimates over invented precision, and a break-even test rather than a fabricated return figure.</li>
          <li>Deliver the same finding in layered versions: full technical detail for the system owner, decision history for the analyst, and a version stripped of every table reference for the executive who has to act on it.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="outcome"><b>Outcome</b> &mdash; technical investment gets funded on its merits, and escalations close with a decision instead of another meeting.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="card">
        <span class="tag">Knowledge Engineering</span>
        <h3>Converting tribal expertise into institutional capability</h3>
        <p class="setup">Undocumented business logic is operational risk wearing a disguise.</p>
        <ul class="b">
          <li>Turn model exports, pipeline code, and scattered decisions into onboarding-grade references: architecture and data-flow documentation, field dictionaries with source logic, metric definitions with lineage.</li>
          <li>Capture domain knowledge as a durable artifact at the moment it is discovered, not reconstructed later from memory.</li>
          <li>Keep investigation logs that preserve why a call was made, not merely what was decided.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="outcome"><b>Outcome</b> &mdash; onboarding shortens, handoffs stop being risky, and the program can answer "how was this calculated" under audit.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="card">
        <span class="tag">Collaboration</span>
        <h3>Aligning definitions, and lifting the people around me</h3>
        <p class="setup">Reporting accuracy is rarely blocked by a technical problem alone.</p>
        <ul class="b">
          <li>Do the cross-functional alignment that makes a definition stick across legal, client-facing, engineering, product, and analytics teams in different time zones.</li>
          <li>Route fixes to the owning team with the evidence pre-assembled, rather than escalating a complaint.</li>
          <li>Mentor analysts into the models and pipelines, and build reference material specifically so colleagues can self-serve.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="outcome"><b>Outcome</b> &mdash; capability scales past a single person, and stakeholders trust both the number and the caveats attached to it.</p>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Technical range</h2>
    <div class="skills">

      <div class="skill-group">
        <h3>Data &amp; Query</h3>
        <div class="chips">
          <span class="chip">SQL</span><span class="chip">Hive</span><span class="chip">Presto</span>
          <span class="chip">Trino</span><span class="chip">Databricks</span>
          <span class="chip">Multi-CTE pipelines</span><span class="chip">Merge logic</span>
          <span class="chip">Lineage analysis</span><span class="chip">ODBC</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="skill-group">
        <h3>BI &amp; Semantic Modeling</h3>
        <div class="chips">
          <span class="chip">Power BI</span><span class="chip">TMDL</span><span class="chip">DAX</span>
          <span class="chip">Power Query (M)</span><span class="chip">PBIR JSON</span>
          <span class="chip">Paginated reports</span><span class="chip">Star schema</span>
          <span class="chip">Row-level security</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="skill-group">
        <h3>Cloud &amp; Lakehouse</h3>
        <div class="chips">
          <span class="chip">Microsoft Fabric</span><span class="chip">OneLake</span>
          <span class="chip">Delta tables</span><span class="chip">Lakehouse endpoints</span>
          <span class="chip">Server-side pipelines</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="skill-group">
        <h3>Automation &amp; Engineering</h3>
        <div class="chips">
          <span class="chip">Python</span><span class="chip">PowerShell</span>
          <span class="chip">Task scheduling</span><span class="chip">COM automation</span>
          <span class="chip">Retry &amp; resilience</span><span class="chip">Structured logging</span>
          <span class="chip">Webhooks</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="skill-group">
        <h3>AI-Augmented Practice</h3>
        <div class="chips">
          <span class="chip">Agentic IDEs</span><span class="chip">Frontier models</span>
          <span class="chip">Agent skills &amp; subagents</span><span class="chip">Guardrails as config</span>
          <span class="chip">Approval-gated automation</span><span class="chip">Usage attribution</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="skill-group">
        <h3>Analysis &amp; Investigation</h3>
        <div class="chips">
          <span class="chip">Root-cause analysis</span><span class="chip">Variance decomposition</span>
          <span class="chip">Reconciliation</span><span class="chip">Hypothesis testing</span>
          <span class="chip">Anomaly &amp; fraud enrichment</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="skill-group">
        <h3>Governance &amp; Data Quality</h3>
        <div class="chips">
          <span class="chip">Fail-closed checks</span><span class="chip">Coverage auditing</span>
          <span class="chip">Drift &amp; freshness monitoring</span><span class="chip">Privacy-safe methodology</span>
          <span class="chip">Ownership routing</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="skill-group">
        <h3>Ways of Working</h3>
        <div class="chips">
          <span class="chip">Git</span><span class="chip">Pull-request review</span>
          <span class="chip">Issue tracking</span><span class="chip">Technical writing</span>
          <span class="chip">Executive communication</span><span class="chip">Documentation</span>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How I operate</h2>
    <ul class="principles">
      <li><b>Reframe before you fix.</b> Challenge the premise before spending a query on the assumed cause.</li>
      <li><b>Make every claim falsifiable.</b> A finding counts only if a specific test could have disproved it.</li>
      <li><b>Privacy-safe by default.</b> Aggregate-first methods; sensitive fields routed to count-only paths.</li>
      <li><b>Report honestly, including against yourself.</b> When the data disproves my theory, that is the finding.</li>
      <li><b>Fix the cause, not the symptom.</b> Padding a timeout or widening a filter buys quiet, not correctness.</li>
      <li><b>Leave a durable artifact.</b> Every investigation ends in documentation, a check, or tooling.</li>
      <li><b>Gate anything irreversible on a human.</b> Automation earns speed everywhere except the decision to commit.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

